deployment modes in spark

Deployment mode determines where the Spark Driver runs. Local mode runs everything on a single machine, Client mode runs the driver on the submitting machine, and Cluster mode runs the driver inside the cluster while executors run on worker nodes.

Local Mode
Driver runs on local machine.
Executors run on local machine.
No cluster required.
Used for learning, development, testing.
Limited by local CPU, RAM, and disk.
Easy debugging and log access.
Not suitable for large-scale processing.

Client Mode
Driver runs on the machine submitting the job.
Executors run on cluster worker nodes.
Cluster required.
Used for development and debugging on a cluster.
Driver logs are visible on the client machine.
Client machine must remain reachable/alive.
Network communication happens between driver and executors.
If client machine crashes, job may fail.

Cluster Mode
Driver runs inside the cluster.
Executors run inside the cluster.
Cluster required.
Most commonly used in production.
Client can disconnect after job submission.
Better fault tolerance and reliability.
Scales well for large workloads.
Driver logs are accessed through Spark/YARN/Dataproc UI.
Preferred for Airflow, Dataproc, EMR, and production ETL pipeline

nterview Points
Catalog stores metadata, not actual data.
Temporary views are registered in Spark Catalog.
Managed tables and databases are tracked through the Catalog.
Spark SQL uses Catalog to resolve table names.
